  "query_preview": "..\n\nA classic split flow pattern remains in place for Sunday as a\ntrough remains situated over the desert southwest with zonal flow\nto the north in advance of a Pacific Northwest closed low. The \ndesert low will lift northeast into the Northern Plains but \nremaining far enough east to keep much of NE Montana dry. An \nexception may be areas along the Yellowstone from Terry to Sidney \nand points to the east where a few rain showers may occur Sunday \nnight into Monday. Persistent SE low level winds on Monday may \nalso create rough conditions out on Fort Peck Lake.\n\nMeanwhile, system ejecting east out of the Pacific northwest will\nrapidly approach Tuesday into Wednesday and will bring widespread\nrainfall to the region during that time. Ensemble mean QPF for the\nperiod right now ranges from about 0.10 to 0.25\" for most\nlocations if not a nudge above. Given the overall trough like \npattern continuing through the week, temperatures will likely \nremain below normal regardless of rainfall chances.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM was used this forecast cycle save for tweaks to Sunday morning\nlows where temperatures were falling more aggressively in a very\ndry air mass in place. Otherwise, excellent consensus on the going\nforecast depicting below normal temperatures through the week with\ndetails coming into focus on precipitation chances for the\nmidweek.",
